title: RFC-145 (former RFC-4) host functions implementation
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    This implements the host functions introduced by [RFC-145](https://github.com/polkadot-fellows/RFCs/pull/145).
    The implementation is gated behind the `rfc145` compile-time `cfg`: without `--cfg rfc145` a runtime
    imports and a node registers exactly the same host functions as before, while the RFC-145 host
    functions, the V2 entry point and the runtime-side allocator are only compiled in with the `cfg`
    set, both for the node and (through `substrate-wasm-builder`) for the runtime.

    The `sp-io` API is the same in both builds, which comes with the following changes for every user,
    including those never enabling the `cfg`:
    - `sp_io::storage::read` is split into `read_exact` and `read_partial` (same for the child storage).
    - `sp_io::storage::next_key` writes the next key into a caller provided `&mut Vec<u8>` and returns
      a `bool` instead of returning an `Option<Vec<u8>>` (same for the child storage).
    - `sp_io::storage::root` and `sp_io::default_child_storage::root` are generic over the hash type
      of the runtime: `root::<H>(version)`.
    - `sp_io::storage::clear_prefix` and the child storage `clear_prefix`/`storage_kill` take an
      optional cursor and return `MultiRemovalResults`; `frame_support::storage::child::kill_storage`
      and `frame_support::storage::unhashed::kill_prefix` are removed in favour of the cursor based
      `clear_storage`/`clear_prefix`.
    - `sp_externalities::Externalities::storage_root` no longer takes the state version, which is set
      through the new `set_runtime_state_version` instead.
